Treat an alternative of -o that holds no tests as true.
An alternative made only of options such as -xdev or -maxdepth got no
action group, and the NULL left in its place ended the list of groups,
so evaluation stopped there: "-name a -o -xdev -o -name b" never tested
"-name b" and matched only "a", "-name a -o -xdev" matched only "a",
and the final -exec + batches after such a gap never ran. GNU find
treats these options as true wherever they appear, so such an
alternative matches every file. Give every alternative a group, empty
when it has no actions, so it is true and the groups after it are kept.
A -o with nothing after it, which GNU find rejects, is now an error
rather than an empty alternative that would match every file.

---
 findutils/find.c |   10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/findutils/find.c
+++ b/findutils/find.c
@@ -1336,10 +1336,16 @@
 		}
 		else if (parm == PARM_o IF_DESKTOP(|| parm == PARM_or)) {
 			dbg("%d", __LINE__);
-			/* start new OR group */
+			if (!arg1) /* "find -name a -o" */
+				bb_error_msg_and_die(bb_msg_requires_arg, arg);
+			/* start new OR group. A group with no actions (only
+			 * options such as -xdev) is true: it must not be left
+			 * NULL, which would end the list of groups */
+			if (!appp[cur_group])
+				appp[cur_group] = xzalloc(sizeof(appp[0][0]));
 			cur_group++;
 			appp = xrealloc(appp, (cur_group+2) * sizeof(appp[0]));
-			/*appp[cur_group] = NULL; - already NULL */
+			appp[cur_group] = xzalloc(sizeof(appp[0][0]));
 			appp[cur_group+1] = NULL;
 			cur_action = 0;
 		}
